// 06_message_queue.zp — Distributed message queue (Kafka-like)
// Conventional: Apache Kafka (~500,000 LOC Java) or RabbitMQ (~100,000 LOC Erlang)
// Codex Labs LLC — 2026

// ── THE INSIGHT ────────────────────────────
//
// A message queue is a wire with memory.
// Z+ wires already flow signals. Vault already stores.
// Kafka is 500,000 lines of Java to simulate a wire with memory.

// ── TOPICS ─────────────────────────────────

// a topic is a named wire
topic("orders")    : wire -> orders
topic("payments")  : wire -> payments
topic("logs")      : wire -> logs

// ── PRODUCE ────────────────────────────────

// producing = emitting a signal onto a named wire
producer : net.listen(port: 9092, proto: tcp) -> incoming

incoming -> gate(topic: "orders")   -> orders
incoming -> gate(topic: "payments") -> payments
incoming -> gate(topic: "logs")     -> logs

// ── CONSUME ────────────────────────────────

// consuming = tapping a wire from a named position
// each consumer group tracks its own position (offset)

orders -> consumer_group("order-service") -> {
    deliver(to: subscriber),
    ack -> advance(offset)
}

orders -> consumer_group("analytics") -> {
    deliver(to: subscriber),
    ack -> advance(offset)
}

// two groups on the same wire. each reads independently.
// that's just... two taps on the same signal.

// ── PERSISTENCE ────────────────────────────

// every topic is stored — the wire has memory
orders   -> vault.append("orders", retention: 7d)
payments -> vault.append("payments", retention: 30d)
logs     -> vault.append("logs", retention: 3d)

// replay = reading from vault instead of live wire
vault.replay("orders", from: offset) -> consumer_group("late-joiner")

// ── PARTITIONING ───────────────────────────

// partition = fork by key, for ordered parallel processing
orders -> partition(by: order_id, count: 8) -> {
    partition(0) -> consumer(0),
    partition(1) -> consumer(1),
    // ...
    partition(7) -> consumer(7)
}

// order guaranteed within partition.
// signals with the same key always flow to the same partition.
// that's just deterministic routing through a fork.

// ── BACKPRESSURE ───────────────────────────

// consumer too slow? signals accumulate. that's a signal too.
consumer_group("order-service").lag -> gate(> 1000, knee: 500) -> {
    alert(warn: "consumer lagging"),
    producer.throttle(0.8)              // tell producers to ease off
}

// lag is just: current offset - consumer offset.
// it's a delta. Z+ already thinks in deltas.

// ── DEAD LETTER ────────────────────────────

// message failed 3 times → dead letter wire
consumer_group -> on_fail(retries: 3) -> dead_letter
dead_letter -> vault.store("dead_letters") ~> alert(error: "dead letter")

// ── TELEMETRY ──────────────────────────────

orders   ~> rate(per: 1s) ~> orders_throughput
payments ~> rate(per: 1s) ~> payments_throughput
consumer_group("order-service").lag ~> consumer_lag
vault    ~> disk_usage(by: topic) ~> storage


// ── FINDINGS ───────────────────────────────
//
// WORKED:  topic as named wire — this is literally what a topic is
//          consumer groups as taps with position tracking — natural
//          persistence as vault.append with retention — temporal storage
//          backpressure as lag signal through a gate — delta thinking
//          dead letter as on_fail with retry count — graceful degradation
//          partitioning as deterministic fork — already in the language
//
// NEEDED:  topic() — named wire declaration
//          consumer_group() — stateful tap with offset tracking
//          ack / advance(offset) — acknowledgment + position advancement
//          vault.append — append-only storage (different from vault.store)
//          vault.replay — read from storage as signal stream
//          partition(by:, count:) — deterministic signal distribution
//          on_fail(retries:) — retry with count before giving up
//          producer.throttle() — backpressure signal to source
//
// KEY INSIGHT: A message queue is a wire with memory and position tracking.
//   Kafka is half a million lines because Java doesn't have wires.
//   Z+ has wires. Adding memory (vault) and position (offset) to a wire
//   gives you Kafka. The entire concept of a message queue is an
//   artifact of languages that can't express signal flow.
//
// NEW CONSTRUCT: "ack" — acknowledgment signal flowing BACKWARD.
//   consumer receives message, processes it, sends ack back.
//   the ack flows upstream to advance the offset.
//   this is the second case (after HTTP) of bidirectional flow.
//   Z+ needs a formal concept of return signals / upstream flow.
//
// CONVENTIONAL: Kafka is 500k LOC Java. RabbitMQ is 100k LOC Erlang.
//   This is 55 lines. With persistence, partitioning, consumer groups,
//   backpressure, dead letters, and replay. Kafka doesn't have
//   knee-based backpressure — it hard-blocks or hard-drops.
